//! fail
//! stderr: fail/ffi/006_socket_signature_conflict.ft:20:30: error: conflicting
// The directive above pins the file the error stands in. The library's file
// carries the note, and this test pins no position in it: the path is the
// --std-dir the run was given and its line moves with std/libc.ft.
// D14.2
//
// std.libc declares the seven socket calls (stdlib.md 2.2) and every closure
// holds std.libc, because every closure holds std.rt and std.rt imports it. So
// D9.8's identity rule binds a program's own declaration of one of those C
// symbols to the library's: the two must be the same types, parameter names
// excepted.
//
// The declaration below is the one a program wrote before std.net existed. C
// takes the address length of accept(2) through a socklen_t*, which is u32 mut*
// and not void*, so this is one ELF symbol reached through two types, which is
// the bug the identity rule exists to catch. The way out is `import std.net;`,
// or `import std.libc;` and a call of libc.accept.
// D9.8, D9.10
extern fn i32 accept(i32 fd, void* addr, void* len); //! error: conflicting declarations of extern 'accept'

// The declaration alone is the error, so main calls nothing: a call would add
// diagnostics about a name the loader has already refused.
fn i32 main() {
    return 0;
}
